(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-dermatology-ii-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Dermatology II Quiz 2 — CMS I Exam 1","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which feature most reliably distinguishes primary from secondary hyperhidrosis?","choices":["Primary disease always has an identifiable underlying cause","Primary disease is bilateral, focal and absent during sleep","Primary disease is generalised and worse at night","Primary disease begins after the age of fifty"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — secondary disease is generalised, may be asymmetric, and can occur at night. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"Which biologic is approved for pyoderma gangrenosum in inflammatory bowel disease?","choices":["Etanercept at 25 to 50 mg subcutaneously","Dupilumab given subcutaneously every two weeks","Infliximab at 5 mg/kg intravenously","Omalizumab at 300 mg subcutaneously every four weeks"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the biologic of choice in that setting, with adalimumab and ustekinumab for refractory cases. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"What is the main differential for localised granuloma annulare, and how is it separated?","choices":["Necrobiosis lipoidica, which has a yellow-waxy atrophic centre on the shins","Rheumatoid nodules, which are firm, periarticular and serology positive","Lichen planus, which is violaceous and polygonal with Wickham striae","Tinea corporis, which has a scaling border and is potassium hydroxide positive"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — granuloma annulare has no scale on its ring. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"How is the diagnosis of pyoderma gangrenosum established?","choices":["By wound culture growing a characteristic organism","By serum antibody testing against dermal structural proteins","By exclusion, since there is no gold standard test","By biopsy showing a pathognomonic palisading granuloma"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — biopsy from the ulcer edge shows dense neutrophils and rules out malignancy and infection. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"What is pathergy, and what does it mean for management?","choices":["Worsening of the ulcer with trauma or debridement, which is why debridement must be avoided","Spread of the ulcer to distant sites through the bloodstream","Failure of the ulcer to culture any organism despite clear clinical signs of infection","Recurrence of the ulcer at the same site after apparent healing"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — this is the single most consequential management point in the condition. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"What is the hardening phenomenon in polymorphous light eruption?","choices":["Loss of pigment in the affected areas after each episode","Improvement through the summer as tolerance develops after repeated exposure","Thickening and hardening of the skin from repeated scratching of the eruption","Progressive scarring of affected sites over successive seasons"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, which is also why prophylactic narrowband ultraviolet B in spring is the most effective preventive strategy. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"What mechanism underlies the most common form of acanthosis nigricans?","choices":["Hyperinsulinaemia stimulating keratinocyte and fibroblast proliferation through insulin-like growth factor 1 receptor cross-activation","A paraneoplastic process driven by an underlying gastric adenocarcinoma secreting growth factors that act on the epidermis","A delayed type IV hypersensitivity reaction to a contacted allergen","Autoantibody deposition along the epithelial basement membrane zone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the insulin-resistant or benign form is the commonest. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"What is the mortality of toxic epidermal necrolysis, and what predicts it at presentation?","choices":["Under 1%, predicted by the number of drugs the patient was taking","Around 50%, predicted by the presence of the Nikolsky sign","Up to 30 to 35%, predicted by the SCORTEN score calculated within 24 hours","Between 1 and 5%, predicted by the extent of mucosal involvement"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and SCORTEN is repeated on day three. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 89"},{"q":"Which investigation is used to look for the sarcoidosis association in erythema nodosum?","choices":["Chest radiograph, seeking bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy","Colonoscopy, seeking mucosal ulceration and skip lesions","Antistreptolysin O titre with a throat culture","Serum protein electrophoresis, seeking a monoclonal band"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — Löfgren syndrome, which has the best prognosis of all sarcoidosis presentations. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"How is the classic erythema multiforme target lesion described?","choices":["Grouped vesicles on an erythematous base following a single dermatome","Three concentric zones: a central dusky or necrotic area, a pale oedematous ring, and an outer erythematous halo","Two concentric zones: a central pale area surrounded by a single wide erythematous ring","A single uniform erythematous plaque with a raised advancing border and a clear centre, without any concentric zones"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and the lesions are distributed symmetrically on acral surfaces — palms, soles and dorsal hands. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What distinguishes solar lentigo from lentigo maligna?","choices":["Solar lentigo appears in early childhood; lentigo maligna appears only after the age of seventy","Solar lentigo appears on covered skin; lentigo maligna on exposed skin","Solar lentigo has uniform pigmentation; lentigo maligna is asymmetric with irregular border and colour","Solar lentigo is raised and rough; lentigo maligna is flat and smooth"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and dermoscopy is essential — solar lentigo shows finger-like projections and a moth-eaten border. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 111"},{"q":"What kind of process is erythema nodosum, histologically?","choices":["A palisading granuloma around degenerating collagen","A dense neutrophilic infiltrate without organisms","A septal panniculitis without vasculitis","A lobular panniculitis with associated vasculitis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the most common form of panniculitis, a delayed hypersensitivity reaction in subcutaneous fat. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"What is the underlying mechanism of dermatitis herpetiformis?","choices":["Immunoglobulin G antibodies directed against the epithelial basement membrane zone of the skin","Immunoglobulin G antibodies against keratinocyte adhesion molecules","Immunoglobulin E mediated mast cell degranulation with histamine release","Immunoglobulin A antibodies against epidermal transglutaminase, depositing in the dermal papillae"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, in genetically susceptible individuals consuming gluten, producing subepidermal blisters. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"How much do tanning beds reduce risk compared with natural sun, and how are they classified?","choices":["They are safer for Fitzpatrick types I and II under supervision","They are acceptable if limited to the months outside peak summer","They are not safer at all, and are classified as Group 1 carcinogens","They halve ultraviolet B exposure while delivering the same ultraviolet A dose"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — there is no safe level of tanning. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 96"},{"q":"What causes phytophotodermatitis, and how does it present?","choices":["A delayed hypersensitivity response to an ultraviolet-induced photoantigen","Furanocoumarins in plants such as lime, celery and parsley plus ultraviolet A, producing linear streaked hyperpigmentation","Bergapten in bergamot oil used in fragrances and cosmetics, producing drip-pattern hyperpigmentation of the neck","A persistent eczematous eruption in chronically exposed skin of older males"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — classically after lime juice and sun exposure, with painful blistering acutely. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 100"},{"q":"Which advice is given about blisters in second-degree sunburn?","choices":["Do not pop them, because intact blisters are protective","Drain them under sterile conditions to reduce infection risk","Apply a high-potency topical corticosteroid directly to them","Cover them with silver sulfadiazine to prevent colonisation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, with cool compresses and early nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs for pain. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 96"},{"q":"How are Stevens-Johnson syndrome, the overlap state and toxic epidermal necrolysis separated?","choices":["By the presence of mucosal involvement, which occurs only in the severe form","By whether the trigger was a drug or an infection in each case","By whether the Nikolsky sign is positive or negative at presentation","By body surface area of epidermal detachment: under 10%, 10 to 30%, and over 30%"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the same disease spectrum divided by extent of detachment. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"Which surveillance is required in recessive dystrophic epidermolysis bullosa?","choices":["Annual squamous cell carcinoma surveillance after the age of ten","Annual colonoscopy from the age of ten to detect bowel involvement","Annual echocardiography to monitor for cardiomyopathy","Annual bone density scanning to monitor for osteoporosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the severe scarring of dystrophic disease carries squamous cell carcinoma risk. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"What distinguishes phototoxicity from photoallergy?","choices":["Phototoxicity is immunologic and requires prior sensitisation before any reaction; photoallergy is non-immunologic, dose-dependent and occurs on the very first exposure","Phototoxicity affects only sun-exposed skin; photoallergy affects only covered skin","Phototoxicity resolves on stopping the drug; photoallergy always resolves immediately too","Phototoxicity is non-immunologic and dose-dependent and occurs on first exposure; photoallergy is immunologic and dose-independent and requires prior sensitisation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — phototoxicity resembles an exaggerated sunburn within hours. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 98"},{"q":"Which variant of granuloma annulare carries the strongest systemic associations?","choices":["Perforating, with central umbilication and extrusion of collagen","Generalised or disseminated, associated with diabetes, thyroid disease, dyslipidaemia and lymphoma","Localised disease, which predominates at about 75% of cases and affects children and young adults","Subcutaneous, occurring almost exclusively in children under six"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and it is more common in adults over forty. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"What does direct immunofluorescence show in erythema multiforme, and why does it matter?","choices":["It shows intercellular immunoglobulin G throughout the epidermis, confirming the diagnosis","It is negative, which distinguishes erythema multiforme from pemphigoid and pemphigus","It shows granular immunoglobulin A in the dermal papillae, confirming the diagnosis","It shows linear immunoglobulin G at the basement membrane zone, confirming the diagnosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Biopsy shows interface dermatitis with vacuolar degeneration of the basal layer and necrotic keratinocytes. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Which features suggest malignant rather than benign acanthosis nigricans?","choices":["Gradual onset over years confined to the posterior neck and axillae","Velvety hyperpigmented plaques appearing only in the inframammary folds","Lesions that wipe away completely with an isopropyl alcohol swab","Rapid onset, extensive involvement, oral mucosal change and tripe palms"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — malignant disease is paraneoplastic, especially with gastric adenocarcinoma. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"What is field cancerisation, and how does it change management?","choices":["Clinically normal skin around the lesions harbours subclinical mutations, so multiple or confluent lesions need field-directed therapy","Lesions spread outward from a single origin, so wide excision is required","Lesions seed distant sites through lymphatics, so staging is required","Lesions recur at exactly the same site after treatment, so repeat cryotherapy at regular intervals is the appropriate response"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — 5-fluorouracil, imiquimod, tirbanibulin or diclofenac treat the field rather than single lesions. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 112"},{"q":"Which molecular event is critical in actinic keratosis?","choices":["Loss of keratinocyte adhesion through autoantibody binding","Reduced filaggrin production impairing the epidermal barrier","Ultraviolet-induced mutation of TP53","Overactivation of the hedgehog signalling pathway"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and the surrounding normal-looking skin harbours subclinical mutations, called field cancerisation. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 112"},{"q":"What is beremagene geperpavec?","choices":["A non-adherent biological dressing designed for fragile skin","A topical gene therapy gel approved in 2023 for dystrophic epidermolysis bullosa","A monoclonal antibody given intravenously for junctional epidermolysis bullosa","An oral small molecule that restores keratin 5 and 14 function"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, accessible through dermatology referral centres and gene therapy trials. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"What is first-line treatment for urticaria?","choices":["Second-generation antihistamines such as cetirizine, loratadine or fexofenadine","A short course of oral prednisone at 40 to 60 mg for five days","Omalizumab given as 300 mg subcutaneously every four weeks","Cyclosporine titrated to response with monitoring of renal function"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and in chronic disease these can be scheduled at up to four times the standard dose. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Which intervention is described as the single most evidence-supported for photoaging?","choices":["Ablative laser resurfacing performed every few years","Daily topical vitamin C at 10 to 20% concentration","Chemical peeling with glycolic or trichloroacetic acid","Daily broad-spectrum sunscreen at SPF 30 or above"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, for both preventing and slowing progression, and beginning in childhood gives maximal lifetime benefit. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 119"},{"q":"What is the most common infectious trigger of erythema nodosum?","choices":["Mycoplasma pneumoniae","Herpes simplex virus","Staphylococcus aureus","Group A Streptococcus"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, alongside Yersinia, tuberculosis, coccidioidomycosis, histoplasmosis and hepatitis B and C. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Which laboratory tests evaluate the commonest cause of acanthosis nigricans?","choices":["Fasting glucose, haemoglobin A1c, fasting insulin and HOMA-IR","Serum protein electrophoresis with antinuclear and antineutrophil antibodies","Antistreptolysin O titre, chest radiograph and interferon gamma release assay","Serum tryptase, complement C4 and C1-esterase inhibitor function"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — these assess insulin resistance, with a lipid panel and body measurements for metabolic syndrome. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Which rosacea complication requires urgent ophthalmology referral?","choices":["Papules and pustules spreading beyond the central face","Ocular rosacea with keratitis, visual symptoms or corneal involvement","Rhinophyma causing nasal airway narrowing over several years","Persistent central facial erythema unresponsive to topical therapy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — untreated ocular disease risks corneal scarring. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 70"}]};
